# Which modules the initramfs carries — the kernel-modules-irf payload.
#
# One path per line, relative to lib/modules/<release>/kernel/. A directory
# takes everything beneath it. A line starting with `-` removes that path from
# what the lines above pulled in. Blank lines and # comments are ignored. A
# listed path that does not exist is skipped, so this survives config changes
# that retire a subsystem.
#
# THE SET MUST BE CLOSED UNDER SYMBOL DEPENDENCIES. install-modules-irf.sh runs
# depmod -e over the result and fails the build if any module imports a symbol
# that neither vmlinux nor another module in the set exports — depmod cannot
# record a dependency it cannot resolve, so such a module would index cleanly
# and fail at boot with "Unknown symbol". When the gate names a module, either
# add the subtree that provides the symbol or, if the module has no business in
# an initramfs, exclude it.
#
# GENERIC, NOT HOSTONLY. This set is the same on every machine. Peios cannot
# build a hostonly initramfs even if it wanted to: peipkg-compose resolves
# offline and runs no install-time side effects, so nothing in the compose path
# may probe the hardware it is composing for. A per-machine initramfs would also
# have to be assembled and signed on the target, since the UKI is signed whole —
# which would put a signing key on every installed machine. Generic is the
# mainstream default anyway (Debian's MODULES=most, Fedora's non-hostonly
# dracut); hostonly is the opt-in, not the norm.
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS FILE: the initramfs only has to reach the root filesystem.
# Anything the real root can load for itself once it is mounted does not belong
# here — GPU, sound, media, wifi, sensors, infiniband. Adding a subsystem here
# costs image size on every machine forever, so the bar is "the boot cannot
# proceed without it", not "a machine might have one".

# --- Reaching the root device -------------------------------------------
# Storage controllers, block devices and the transports they sit behind.
drivers/ata
drivers/block
drivers/nvme
drivers/scsi
drivers/md
drivers/message
drivers/mmc
drivers/firewire
drivers/mtd
drivers/nvmem
drivers/virtio
drivers/usb
drivers/pci
# Two PCI controller drivers import from outside drivers/pci: pci-hyperv from
# the Hyper-V VMBus (a Hyper-V guest reaches its disk through it), and
# pci-pwrctrl-slot from the power-sequencing helpers. Both small.
drivers/hv
drivers/power/sequencing
# virtio_vdpa (drivers/virtio) is a virtio transport over vDPA devices; the
# vDPA bus in turn pulls the vhost ring helpers and a mlx5 back-end, so the
# transport goes rather than the bus coming in.
-drivers/virtio/virtio_vdpa.ko.zst
# The PCMCIA SCSI adapters under drivers/scsi import the PCMCIA core.
drivers/pcmcia
# Parallel port. Not itself a way to a root, but lp, ppdev, the ppa/imm SCSI
# bridges, pata_parport and two USB adapters all import it, and the core is
# two small modules — cheaper to carry than to list every consumer.
drivers/parport
# bcma-hcd (a USB host on the Broadcom BCMA bus) imports the bus driver.
drivers/bcma
# The ChromeOS embedded controller: a Chromebook's keyboard (cros_ec_keyb,
# under drivers/input) speaks to it, so a passphrase prompt there needs it.
drivers/platform/chrome
-drivers/platform/chrome/cros_typec_switch.ko.zst
-drivers/platform/chrome/cros-ec-typec.ko.zst
# The Surface aggregator, for the same reason: a Surface's keyboard is a
# surface-hid device on it.
drivers/platform/surface
-drivers/platform/surface/surface_platform_profile.ko.zst
-drivers/platform/surface/surface3-wmi.ko.zst
# An EEPROM behind the QNAP MCU.
-drivers/nvmem/nvmem-qnap-mcu-eeprom.ko.zst
# 9p over RDMA (drivers/infiniband), vsock over VMCI (the VMware guest
# stack), UIO on the DFL FPGA bus, the AMD TEE (the CCP under drivers/crypto).
-net/9p/9pnet_rdma.ko.zst
-net/sunrpc/xprtrdma
-net/rds/rds_rdma.ko.zst
-net/smc
# Qualcomm IPC router transports over MHI and SMD (rpmsg).
-net/qrtr/qrtr-mhi.ko.zst
-net/qrtr/qrtr-smd.ko.zst
-net/vmw_vsock/vmw_vsock_vmci_transport.ko.zst
-drivers/uio/uio_dfl.ko.zst
-drivers/tee/amdtee
# Single-module providers other SCSI drivers import: ses needs the enclosure
# class, wd719x the 93cx6 EEPROM reader, qedi (iSCSI offload) the UIO core.
drivers/misc/enclosure.ko.zst
drivers/misc/eeprom/eeprom_93cx6.ko.zst
# The SD/MMC hosts under drivers/mmc for the Alcor and TI FlashMedia readers
# sit on these bridge drivers.
drivers/misc/cardreader
drivers/misc/tifm_core.ko.zst
drivers/misc/tifm_7xx1.ko.zst
drivers/misc/cb710
# mmc_block — every eMMC/SD root — imports the RPMB core.
drivers/misc/rpmb-core.ko.zst
drivers/uio
# b44 (Broadcom 44xx Ethernet, under drivers/net) sits on the SSB bus.
drivers/ssb
# Small cores the Intel and Myricom NICs import: igb the bit-banging I2C
# algorithm, ixgbe and myri10ge direct cache access, ice the GNSS class.
drivers/i2c/algos
drivers/dca
drivers/gnss
drivers/base
drivers/char

# --- Unlocking it -------------------------------------------------------
# Keyboards, so a human can type a passphrase for an encrypted root. USB
# keyboards also need the host controllers above.
drivers/hid
drivers/input

# --- Mounting it --------------------------------------------------------
# Filesystem drivers, the crypto the encrypted case needs, and the shared
# helpers (crc, compression) both pull in.
fs
crypto
lib
arch
# dm-crypt imports the trusted and encrypted key types from security/keys,
# and the trusted type can be backed by a TEE.
security
drivers/tee
# Excluded from the above: modules whose imports the set does not provide and
# which no root-reaching path needs.
#   fs/smb        cifs links the RDMA stack (drivers/infiniband)
#   fs/ecryptfs   the `encrypted` key type (security/keys)
#   arch/x86/kvm  SEV firmware calls (drivers/crypto/ccp); a hypervisor is
#                 not something the initramfs runs
#   fs/vboxsf     the VirtualBox guest driver (drivers/virt/vboxguest)
-fs/smb
-fs/ecryptfs
-fs/vboxsf
-arch/x86/kvm
#   lpfc  Emulex Fibre Channel HBA; imports the NVMe-over-FC target side
#         (excluded above). An FC-attached root is not a case Peios has met.
-drivers/scsi/lpfc
#   SCSI target-mode drivers (the machine serving disks, not reading them):
#   tcm_qla2xxx and efct import the target core (drivers/target)
-drivers/scsi/qla2xxx/tcm_qla2xxx.ko.zst
-drivers/scsi/elx/efct.ko.zst
#   drivers/block/rnbd   network block device over RDMA (drivers/infiniband)
#   nvme-rdma            NVMe over RDMA fabrics, same stack
-drivers/block/rnbd
-drivers/nvme/host/nvme-rdma.ko.zst
#   drivers/nvme/target  NVMe target mode: serving namespaces, not reading them
-drivers/nvme/target
#   regmap back-ends for buses the set does not carry (w1, slimbus, soundwire)
-drivers/base/regmap/regmap-w1.ko.zst
-drivers/base/regmap/regmap-slimbus.ko.zst
-drivers/base/regmap/regmap-sdw.ko.zst
-drivers/base/regmap/regmap-sdw-mbq.ko.zst

# --- Network ------------------------------------------------------------
# For a root filesystem reached over the network. Peios has no netboot or NFS
# root today — live-boot mounts the boot medium, disk-boot mounts a disk — so
# these two lines currently serve a root type that does not exist, and they are
# 49 MB and 1182 modules of the set, more than the rest combined. Deleting them
# is the single biggest size lever here. They are kept because MODULES=most is
# defined to include them and dropping them is a decision about which root
# types Peios intends to support, not a tidy-up.
drivers/net
# The rule at the top says no wireless, and drivers/net would otherwise bring
# it: a wireless root needs a supplicant and credentials the initramfs does not
# have. Amateur-radio and parallel-port network drivers go for the same
# reason as lp above.
-drivers/net/wireless
-drivers/net/hamradio
-drivers/net/plip
# WWAN modems (MHI bus), CAN bus and MCTP: not paths to a root filesystem.
-drivers/net/wwan
-drivers/net/mhi_net.ko.zst
-drivers/net/can
-drivers/net/mctp
# Networking over NTB and over Thunderbolt: each wants its bus (drivers/ntb,
# drivers/thunderbolt), neither reaches a root.
-drivers/net/ntb_netdev.ko.zst
-drivers/net/thunderbolt
# USB device (gadget) mode — the machine as a peripheral. The UDC core stays
# because the dual-role host controllers (dwc2, dwc3, isp1760) import it; the
# gadget functions and legacy composites go. Plus the Nokia Tahvo PHY, which
# wants the Retu MFD.
-drivers/usb/gadget/function
-drivers/usb/gadget/legacy
-drivers/usb/phy/phy-tahvo.ko.zst
# USB Type-C port management wants USB4 (drivers/thunderbolt); a keyboard
# on a Type-C port works without it. Input devices read through IIO ADC
# channels and the iqs62x MFD are embedded-board parts.
-drivers/usb/typec
-drivers/input/joystick/adc-joystick.ko.zst
-drivers/input/touchscreen/resistive-adc-touch.ko.zst
-drivers/input/keyboard/adc-keys.ko.zst
-drivers/input/keyboard/iqs62x-keys.ko.zst
# The Atmel maXTouch touchscreen exposes a V4L2 raw-data device (drivers/media).
-drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_mxt_ts.ko.zst
-drivers/input/touchscreen/sur40.ko.zst
# mc13783_ts reads the Freescale PMIC's ADC (drivers/mfd); tsc2007 and the
# Colibri VF50 go through IIO.
-drivers/input/touchscreen/mc13783_ts.ko.zst
-drivers/input/touchscreen/tsc2007.ko.zst
-drivers/input/touchscreen/colibri-vf50-ts.ko.zst
# drivers/input/misc is power buttons, haptics and board-specific parts on
# MFDs the set does not carry; a passphrase comes in through keyboard/.
-drivers/input/misc
# A PTP clock on the DFL FPGA bus.
-drivers/ptp/ptp_dfl_tod.ko.zst
# HID quirk drivers that import sound, IIO, LED or vendor-platform code; a
# keyboard on any of these still works through hid-generic.
-drivers/hid/hid-asus.ko.zst
-drivers/hid/hid-prodikeys.ko.zst
-drivers/hid/hid-mcp2221.ko.zst
-drivers/hid/hid-lg-g15.ko.zst
-drivers/hid/hid-playstation.ko.zst
-drivers/hid/hid-picolcd.ko.zst
# Not a network driver, but Intel's wired drivers (e1000e, igb, ixgbe, ice) all
# import ptp_clock_register from it, and a module whose symbols are missing
# fails to load with "Unknown symbol" — found when the initramfs coldplug hook
# (PEI-58) tried e1000e on q35's emulated NIC. depmod over a subset cannot
# catch this: it drops the unresolvable dependency instead of naming a missing
# file, so install-modules-irf.sh's dangling-reference check passes. If the
# network block above ever goes, this line goes with it.
drivers/ptp
# ptp in turn imports pps_register_source from here. Same story one level
# down; the two go together.
drivers/pps
net
